content:
{
  "asset": "Pump P-113 - AquaDyne HF-300 end-suction centrifugal pump, serial HF3-22650 (1480 rpm, 55 kW, 400 V motor, replaced yesterday)",
  "priority": "P2",
  "stop_equipment_now": false,
  "condition_assessment": [
    {
      "statement": "The drive-end vibration of 4.8 mm/s is in Zone C (4.5 to 7.1 mm/s RMS), labelled 'Restricted - plan maintenance'. The limit is Figure 3 of the HF-300 manual. The reading is 0.3 mm/s above the Zone B/C boundary of 4.5 mm/s and 2.3 mm/s below the Zone D stop threshold of 7.1 mm/s. The manual says to classify the highest reading from both bearing housings and act on that zone, so maintenance should be planned, but an immediate stop is not required.",
      "citations": [
        "C07",
        "C06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "The bearing housing temperature of 70 C is exactly at the upper limit of the normal range (normal up to 70 C). It is 10 C below the 80 C alarm and 20 C below the 90 C trip. The limit is the manual specification table, with temperature measured on the housing surface. Because it is at the edge of normal, the temperature must be trended.",
      "citations": [
        "C02",
        "C01"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "No leakage is reported, so the seal-leakage symptom does not apply and seal replacement is not required.",
      "citations": [
        "C09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "The pump does not meet the stop criteria. Vibration is below Zone D (7.1 mm/s) and temperature is below alarm (80 C) and trip (90 C). The pump may run under restriction until planned alignment work is done.",
      "citations": [
        "C07",
        "C02"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "probable_causes": [
    {
      "statement": "Shaft misalignment between the new motor and the pump is the most likely cause. The rise in vibration started right after the motor replacement. The manual lists misalignment as the first cause of high vibration and as a cause of high bearing temperature. It also requires laser alignment after any work that moves the motor.",
      "citations": [
        "C09",
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Bearing wear is a secondary possibility, supported by the elevated vibration and a housing temperature at the normal limit.",
      "citations": [
        "C09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Impeller imbalance or damage is less likely, since only the motor was disturbed.",
      "citations": [
        "C09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Cavitation (insufficient NPSH) is less likely and should be checked only if the vibration persists after alignment.",
      "citations": [
        "C09"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tasks": [
    {
      "statement": "Before stopping the pump, measure vibration velocity (mm/s RMS) on both the drive-end and non-drive-end bearing housings. Classify the highest reading against Figure 3. If any reading is 7.1 mm/s or above (Zone D), stop the pump immediately.",
      "citations": [
        "C06",
        "C07"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Record the bearing housing surface temperature on both housings. If either reaches the 80 C alarm or the 90 C trip, stop the pump.",
      "citations": [
        "C02",
        "C01"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Stop and isolate the pump to carry out the alignment.",
      "citations": [
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Perform a laser shaft alignment between the motor and the pump, as required after any work that moves the motor. Tolerances are a parallel offset of 0.05 mm or less and an angular misalignment of 0.05 mm per 100 mm or less. Record the as-found and as-left values.",
      "citations": [
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "If the bearing housing bolts (M12) are disturbed, retighten them to 85 Nm with lightly oiled threads, per Table 5.",
      "citations": [
        "C11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Restart the pump and re-measure vibration on both housings. The target is Zone B (4.5 mm/s or less) or better. Also confirm the housing temperature is 70 C or less.",
      "citations": [
        "C07",
        "C02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "If vibration is still in Zone C after alignment, inspect the bearings and impeller and check NPSH, following the troubleshooting table.",
      "citations": [
        "C09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "If either bearing shows wear, noise or high temperature, replace both bearings as a pair (DE 6312-C3 and NDE 6310-C3). Retighten the bearing housing bolts to 85 Nm.",
      "citations": [
        "C10",
        "C05",
        "C11"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "parts": [
    {
      "part_number": "HF3-512",
      "description": "Drive-end bearing 6312-C3. Contingent: use only if inspection shows wear, noise or high temperature. Replace as a pair with HF3-510.",
      "quantity": 1,
      "citations": [
        "C05",
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_number": "HF3-510",
      "description": "Non-drive-end bearing 6310-C3. Contingent: use only if bearing replacement is required. Replace as a pair with HF3-512.",
      "quantity": 1,
      "citations": [
        "C05",
        "C10"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "safety_controls": [
    {
      "statement": "Isolate the pump before any work on the pump or the coupling.",
      "citations": [
        "C10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Never run the pump dry, because the seal flush is Plan 11 from the pump discharge. Make sure the pump is primed before any test run.",
      "citations": [
        "C02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "If the back pull-out assembly has to be removed for bearing or impeller inspection, it weighs 68 kg and lifting equipment is required.",
      "citations": [
        "C02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Run-time stop criteria while the pump operates in Zone C: vibration of 7.1 mm/s RMS or above (Zone D, damage likely), or housing temperature of 80 C (alarm) or 90 C (trip).",
      "citations": [
        "C07",
        "C02"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "labour": [
    {
      "statement": "Laser alignment: 1.5 hours, 1 technician.",
      "citations": [
        "C12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "statement": "Contingent bearing replacement (pair): 6.0 hours, 2 technicians.",
      "citations": [
        "C12"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "source_conflicts": [
    {
      "statement": "The manual and Service Bulletin SB-2026-014 (issued 14 March 2026) disagree on the mechanical seal. The manual parts list specifies seal HF3-450, and Table 5 gives a seal gland nut torque of 25 Nm. The bulletin says that pumps with serial HF3-22000 and above must not use HF3-450. They must use cartridge seal HF3-450B, with a gland torque of 30 Nm, and sleeve HF3-455 must be replaced if scoring is deeper than 0.1 mm. Serial HF3-22650 is above HF3-22000, so the bulletin applies to P-113. This does not affect the current job, as no leakage is reported. However, any future seal work on this pump must use HF3-450B at 30 Nm.",
      "citations": [
        "C13",
        "C05",
        "C11"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "open_questions": [
    "Did the electrical contractor perform and record a laser alignment after fitting the new motor? What were the as-left values?",
    "What is the non-drive-end vibration reading, and is the reported 4.8 mm/s an RMS velocity measured on the bearing housing, as Figure 3 requires?",
    "What was the vibration and temperature baseline before the motor change? Is the 70 C housing temperature stable or rising, and which housing was it measured on?",
    "The evidence gives no torque values for the motor foot bolts or coupling bolts, and no guidance on soft-foot checks.",
    "The evidence does not cover checking the new motor itself (motor vibration limits, rotation direction, motor bearing condition).",
    "The evidence gives no detailed lockout/tagout procedure beyond 'isolate'. Site LOTO rules must be confirmed."
  ]
}
